{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788161285,"nonce":"jfDL34tFx6F9tSU1ZAoshd9GyK4=","title":"ci: `test` and `coverage` run the entire Rust suite twice on every push (~18 min)","message":"`ci.yml` runs the whole Rust suite twice on every push:\n\n* `test` — `cargo nextest run --workspace --cargo-quiet`\n* `coverage` — `cargo llvm-cov nextest --workspace --ignore-filename-regex … --lcov`\n\n`cargo llvm-cov nextest` *runs the tests*; the `--ignore-filename-regex` only\nshapes the report, not the run. So the second job re-executes the same suite\nunder instrumentation, and each job independently stands up its own Postgres\nservice and its own MinIO container first.\n\nMeasured on PR #3: `test` 9.0 min, `coverage` 9.2 min. ~18 of the PR's 27\nrunner-minutes, on a PR that changed no Rust at all.\n\nTwo ways out, and the choice is a real trade, not an obvious win:\n\n* **Merge them.** One instrumented run produces both signals; saves ~9 min on\n  every Rust PR. Costs: the required-check names change (so\n  `.github/required-checks.txt` and any muscle memory move with them), and a\n  plain test failure now surfaces as a `coverage` failure — the test signal\n  becomes coupled to the coverage tooling.\n* **Leave both, let path filters do the work** (see the sibling ticket). A\n  UI/docs PR then pays neither, but a Rust PR still pays double.\n\nWorth deciding alongside the path-filter ticket rather than before it: if the\nfilters land first, this stops mattering for the majority of PRs and the\nremaining case is Rust-only changes.","files":null}]}